//   Online Bible before this data is used in any project.
//
// $$$
//	These definitions refer to J. Green's A Concise Lexicon of the Biblical Languages (CLBL) and Harris, Archer, & Waltke's Theological Wordbook of the Old Testament (TWOT),). 
//	These have been edited to conform with OpenScripture's extended Strongs by Tyndale House Cambridge. Alignment with dStrongs is not yet completed. 
//
//
//
// Data structure
// ==============
//
// Each record is keyed upon a dStrong value (see below for an explanation of
// the various alternative Strongs numbers), and contains the following fields:
//
// - eStrong.  See below.
//
// - uStrong.  See below.
//
// - Ancient: Unicode lexical form.  In JSON files containing Greek words, this
//   is based on LSJ but conforming to BADG when the difference may be confusing.
//   In JSON files containing Hebrew words, the field is based upon BDB, and is
//   normalised.
//
// - Transliteration: Transliteration of the Greek into Roman characters.
//
// - Morph: See below.
//
// - Gloss: Meaning in as few words as possible (by Tyndale Scholars).
//
// - Meaning: Extended meaning.  In some (but not all) files, the meaning is
//   marked with the source from which the  meaning was taken as shown below.
//   (AS and ML are used in brief lexicon data, LSJ in full lexicon data.)
//
//   . (AS): Abbott Smith, from https://github.com/translatable-exegetical-tools/Abbott-Smith,
//     with corrections and adapted by Tyndale Scholars.
//
//   . (ML): Middle Liddell, from Perseus.  Used where there is no entry in AS.
//
//   . (LSJ) Full LSJ from Perseus, with additional features and corrections by
//     Tyndale House, Cambridge:
//
//     + Most abbreviations have been expanded, usually in accordance with the
//       introductions to the lexicons but occasionally made clearer.
//
//     + Dates have been added to authors by Tyndale House, using standard
//       reference works.
//
//     + Coding is added to hide bibliographic data under a link stating the
//       earliest date included in that data. (Note, not all devices work well
//       with tooltips, so consider implementing a clickable option such as
//       shown at http://jsfiddle.net/xaAN3/.)
//
//
//
// Strongs numbers
// ===============
//
// Strongs numbers here come in three flavours -- eStrong (extended Strongs),
// dStrong (STEPBible's disambiguated Strongs), and uStrong (STEPBible's
// unified Strongs).  A lexicon or tagging system will normally use only one set,
// depending on its purpose, though it may be useful to keep both dStrong & uStrong.
//
// Each system has its own advantages and disadvantages:
//
// eStrong is the standard Extended Strongs which conforms with the subdivisions of
// words in BDB (Brown Driver Brigg's lexicon), largely followed by later lexicons.
// The work here was carried out by OS (OpenScriptures.org).
//
// dStrong has separate numbers when a given name is used for different people or
// places, and when a word has significantly different sub-meanings. See
// TinyURL.com/DisambiguatedStrongs.
//
// uStrong has a single number covering all of an individual's various names, and
// a single number for a word that has different forms or spellings or that has a
// Hebrew and Aramaic version.
//
// For example,	Strong used G2264 for all three people in the NT named Herod
// (the Great, Anitipas and Agrippa I), and a different number for 'Herodian',
// which describes Herod Anitipas:
//
// - eStrong does nott divide up any names in the NT (in contrast to the way things
//   work in the OT) eStrong was created to map Strongs to the Hebrew lexicon BDB.
//
// - dStrong has a unique number for each person: G2264G, G2264H, G2264I.  As in
//   the OT, letters start at G when there is no "a" or "b" etc in eStrongs
//   (which never happens in Greek).
//
// - uStrong has only one unique number for each individual, so a search for that
//   number finds all the places where that person is mentioned no matter how their
//   name is spelled.
//
// See further discussion at TinyURL.com/STEP-Disambiguated.
//
//
// eStrongs numbers are as follows:
//
// - G0001 - G5624: The Strongs numbers from original set made by Strong for the
//   text behind the KJV.
//
// - G3203 - G3302: Not used for some reason.
//
// - G5627 - G5799: Greek morphology codes (not used in this lexicon).
//
// - G5800 - G5893: Greek Synonyms - see http://studybible.info/strongs/G5800
//   (not used in this lexicon).
//
// - G6000 - G6090: Extra Strongs Numbers in NASB mainly for variants not known
//   to Strong.  (They remain the same for the sake of backward compatibility
//   with NASB.)
//
// - G6100 - G9979: Extra Greek from Apostolic Bible Project, mainly for LXX
//   words not found in NT (including Greek names that do not occur in the Hebrew).
//
// - G6091 - G6099, G9980 - G9999: Extra Greek for variants which are not included
//   in NASB or Apostolic Bible.
//
// - G10000 - 19999: Greek equivalent of Strongs Hebrew numbers for names and
//   transliterated words eg Abagtha (H0005) = G10005. When there is a NT
//   equivalent, this is the STEPBible lexical tag.
//
// - G20000 - G20199: Words where the Full LSJ entry refers to the LXX, but
//   which are not referred to in the AB tagging because they occur only in
//   non-Protestant canons or in MSS other than Vaticanus (used by AB).
//
//
//
//
// Morphology
// ==========
//
// The 'morph' field gives the simple gramatical value of the main word,
// represented as Language:Type-Gender-Extra.
//
// - Language: A=Aramaic, H=Hebrew, G=Greek and N=Name (this latter
//   option being not language specific).
//
// - Type is A=Adjective, Adv=Adverb, Art=Article, Cond=Conditional,
//   Conj=Conjunction, Cor=Correlative, DemP=Demonstrative Pn,
//   ImpP=Impersonal Pn, Intg=Interrogative, Intj=Interjection,
//   N=Noun, Neg=Negative, Part=Particle, Prep=Preposition,
//   PerP=Personal Pn, PosP=Possessive Pn, RefP=Reflexive Pn,
//   RelP=Relative Pn, V=Verb.
//
// - Gender is F=Female, M=Male, N=Neuter, C=Common, and P or S is
//    optionally added for Plural or Singular.
//
// - 'Extra' for Names is L=Location, P=Person, LG/PG=Gentilic, T=Title
//   (ie any other capitalised nouns such as titles, months, gods, planets,
//   etc).
//
//
// 'Location' is only used for places not named after people -- eg
// Israel = N:N-M-P (for the person and nation); Jerusalem = N:N--L;
// Canaanite is N:N-M-LG; Israelitess is N:N-F-PG.
//
// Location is a geographical entity such as river, mountain or city
// (eg 'Dan', but Gentilic is used for Amon, Syria, Judah, or the tribal
// area of Dan. The idea is that someone from Dan is probably related to
// Dan unless he is called a Hittite, but someone from Jerusalem or the
// city of Dan could be from anywhere even though he is named after the
// city.  So a 'Shulemite' (who is probably from Salem ie Shalem aka
// Jerusalem) is dark-skinned and perhaps not a native from the area.
//
// 'Title' is a miscellaneous category that includes names of gods, musical
// terms and months and offices such as Pharaoh -- unless referring to an
// individual eg Pharaoh Necho who is N:N-M-P.
//
// When F or M occurs with L or T it refers to the gender of that person,
// not the location, so Jerusalem = N:N--L, Jerusalemites = N:N-G-L,
// Jerusalemitess = N:N-F-L.
//
// When Gentilics related to a person the STEP_LexicalTag points to the Person
// eg Danites => Dan.  When Gentilics point to a place, the STEP_LexicalTag
// points to that place, eg Gileadites => Gilead.  When there is no numbered
// tag for the person or place, we probably aren't sure what it is, so the
// STEP_LexicalTag doesn't have anywhere different to point, eg. Girgashites.
//
// Some very common Gentilics (eg Levite) have gained a meaning of their
// own which may be related to a person but much less so - so the STEP_LexicalTag
// does not redirect to the entry for that person.
//
// Odd ones: Jew=N:N-G-P, Jewish=N:A, Jewess+Ammonitess=N:N-G-P-F,
// Canaanite-trader=N:N-G-P / N:N-G-T, Chaldean-magi=N:N-G-L / N:N-G-T,
// Nephusim=N:N-G-T, Rephaim=N:N-G-P / N:N-G-T, Shulamite+Shunammite+Carmelitess+Jezreelitess=N:N-F-L.
//
//
//
// 'Meaning' field
// ===============
//
// The meaning field contains some tags:
//
// - <b>...</b> suggests that the enclosed text should be highlighted in some
//   way.  (The STEPBible project uses boldface here, which is why we have
//   chosen this particular tag.)
//
// - <BR /> indicates a line break.
//
// - <ref> ... </ref> marks Bible references.  As an example:
//
//     <ref ref='Heb.5.4; 7.11; 9.4'>Heb.5:4, 7:11, 9:4</ref>
//
//  The ref attribute gives the target references in 
//
// 
//
//******************************************************************************

",

"suffix": "",



    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Want to convert TSV format to JSON format.
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    
    "tsvToJson": true,


    
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Pattern match to let you recognise the header row, the first data row and
    // the last data row.
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    
    "tsvHeaderRegex": "^eStrong\\tdStrong.*",
    "tsvDataRowLowRegex": "^G0001\\tG0001G.*",
    "tsvDataRowHighRegex": "^G21502\\tG21502\\s+=.*",



    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Field descriptors.
    //--------------------------------------------------------------------------

    "tsvFieldDefinitions": [
	{ "origName": "dStrong", "useAsKey": true, "retainInData": true },
	{ "origName": "Abbott-Smith lexicon (AS), with gaps occationally filled from edited versions of  Middle LSJ", "revisedName": "Meaning" }
    ]
}
